        { "$comment": "P69 round 4 (lane cs): GNU `vector_size(N)` makes the declared type a VECTOR — another size, another alignment, element-wise arithmetic — and this compiler has no vector types. REFUSED BY NAME, at every position the attribute can be written. MEASURED before this row: `typedef int v4 __attribute__((vector_size(16)));` drew a warning that the attribute was unknown and `sizeof(v4)` was 4, where gcc 13.3.0, clang 18.1.3, Apple clang and mingw-w64 gcc 13.2.0 all give 16 — a warning beside a wrong size is still a wrong size, and a program that compiles with one is a program that runs with the other. The `unsupported` verb carries no `appliesTo`: its answer does not depend on what is declared.", "names": ["vector_size"], "effect": "unsupported", "reason": "it makes the declared type a vector of that many bytes, with element-wise arithmetic, and this compiler has no vector types" },
